WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.880
Thanks to KiwiCo for supporting this&nbsp;
episode of Journey to the Microcosmos!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:15.920
Click the link in the description to&nbsp;
learn more and for a special offer.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:24.480
The paramecium is the consummate model organism.&nbsp;
It’s a protozoan that is both easy to grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.200
and easy to study, which means that&nbsp;
microbiologists have been able to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.800
all sorts of secrets about eukaryotic life&nbsp;
by watching them. You might even call the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:42.640
paramecium a hero in our pursuit of knowledge.
But just as heroes in stories have enemies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.400
the paramecium has its own foe. And it&nbsp;
is this strange critter. No, not the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:54.320
eggplant-shaped looking thing. The smaller one,&nbsp;
the one that looks a bit like a swimming okra.&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:01.200
This is didinium, though it wasn’t always called&nbsp;
that. When Otto Friedrich Müller first described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.840
it in 1786, he thought he was observing a&nbsp;
Vorticella, and so he named it Vorticella nasuta.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:13.440
Almost a century later, Samuel Friedrich&nbsp;
Stein would change its name to Didinium.&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.760
Like its paramecium foil, the didinium is a&nbsp;
ciliate. But at first glance, it doesn’t seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:26.160
like it should be much of a problem. For&nbsp;
one thing, paramecium can just get so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.200
larger than a didinium.
Plus, the didinium is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.040
just weird-looking. In fact, between the&nbsp;
paramecium and the didinium, the didinium is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:40.960
the one that actually looks like food. We compared&nbsp;
it to an okra earlier, but at other times it looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:47.680
more like a stretched out acorn. And the pointier&nbsp;
bit looks like a jalapeño stem. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:54.560
it is not a stem, it is the didinium’s proboscis,&nbsp;
which means that is the front of the organism.&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:01.200
And there’s still plenty of weird to go.&nbsp;
The didinium has a long macronucleus that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.720
curves into a sort of figure eight shape.&nbsp;
And circling the organism’s body are two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.440
rows of cilia called pectinelles&nbsp;
that seem almost randomly placed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.160
One row sits at the border of the&nbsp;
proboscis and the rest of the body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.200
while the other clinches the middle like a&nbsp;
loose belt. It’s a little bit tough to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:27.360
the pectinelles in their full fringy glory with&nbsp;
our microscope—they look more like 4 bundles of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.320
hair on the sides of the didinium. But you can see&nbsp;
what it looks like better in this illustration.&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:39.440
And those cilia give didinium both its&nbsp;
very fast and very strange movement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.320
When the didinium moves, it really moves—but&nbsp;
not necessarily in a way that seems obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.160
to us at first glance. Just trying to follow&nbsp;
it with a camera feels a little stressful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.480
The first time James found one, it slipped&nbsp;
so quickly in and out of view that he barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.120
caught a glimpse. He had to switch to a lower&nbsp;
magnification so he could zoom out and see it.&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:08.160
But for the didinium, this seemingly&nbsp;
out-of-control movement is very deliberate. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:15.520
constantly rotating in a clockwise fashion, but&nbsp;
also leaning towards one side so that it’s moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:22.560
in a spiral path. In the early 20th century,&nbsp;
scientists added ink to jelly just so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:30.400
could slow the didinium down and follow this path.
The microbe’s seemingly aimless trajectory helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:37.280
the didinium cover a wide range space in a&nbsp;
short amount of time. Think of it compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:43.680
to another skilled hunter we watched recently, the&nbsp;
lacrymaria olor, which sends its neck out into all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:50.560
directions to maximize the odds of encountering&nbsp;
something. The didinium is doing something similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.520
but instead of extending its neck, it’s sending&nbsp;
its whole body into different directions.&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:03.920
And that speed is a big reason why didinium is&nbsp;
so effective. As it zips around the microcosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.160
it will bump into things that don’t&nbsp;
happen to be food. And when it does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:15.840
it politely backs up and moves on.
But if its proboscis makes contact with something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:23.760
edible, then the didinium goes to work seizing&nbsp;
its prey with its proboscis, paralyzing it, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.360
then eating it. And while it is known to consume&nbsp;
other organisms, what didinium really really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:37.600
likes to eat are paramecium. It doesn’t matter&nbsp;
that a paramecium might be six times its size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:45.840
the didinium is here for a meal and it will gladly&nbsp;
expand itself around the paramecium to swallow it.&nbsp;

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:54.720
But as you can see, this is not a smooth process.&nbsp;
The didinium is still moving and bumping around as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.800
it consumes the paramecium, and for a while, it’s&nbsp;
hard to even tell where predator ends and the prey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:08.240
begins. The only real didinium-like thing about&nbsp;
it is the spiral path it still takes as it eats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:15.440
and eats and eats. But eventually it settles&nbsp;
back into its more awkward-looking self.&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.840
So incredible is the didinium’s appetite&nbsp;
that scientists have observed it eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:27.840
a pair of dividing paramecium. They’ve&nbsp;
also occasionally been observed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.160
exploding from eating too much. So&nbsp;
there are hazards to this lifestyle.&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:41.200
But while the didinium is extremely talented at&nbsp;
hunting paramecium, and also capable of hunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:48.400
other organisms, it’s actually pretty bad in some&nbsp;
of its other conquests. When didinium try to eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:55.520
rotifers, they’re stymied by the relatively tough&nbsp;
exterior. And other ciliates like stentors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.560
too large and too tough and too active&nbsp;
for the didinium to do much damage to.&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:08.400
And paired with its favorite prey, the didinium is&nbsp;
its own model system. In the early 20th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:15.600
mathematicians came up with equations to describe&nbsp;
how populations of predators and prey oscillate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:22.480
in response to each other. More predators means&nbsp;
less prey, which then means fewer predators and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:28.960
more prey. Lather, rinse, and repeat, and you&nbsp;
get a cycle that shapes relationships between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.920
species in many different environments.
And in attempting to see if they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:40.400
re-create these equations that were for the&nbsp;
macroscopic world in a microscopic environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:46.480
scientists in labs turned to paramecium and&nbsp;
didinium to act as a model predator-prey system.&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.920
It turned out to be not so straightforward&nbsp;
to mimic nature in the lab.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.600
When scientists first started watching didinium&nbsp;
and paramecium in a flask, the didinium would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:06.000
inevitably consume the paramecium to extinction,&nbsp;
in turn driving their own deaths. It was only by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:12.240
building on these experiments and finding ways to&nbsp;
adjust the interactions between predator and prey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:17.360
that they could reconstruct the&nbsp;
oscillations we expect in nature.&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:21.760
Of course, that doesn’t mean they directly&nbsp;
copied what nature does. How could they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.880
with an experimental design meant to strip&nbsp;
many of the confounding elements of ecology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:33.200
from their observations? But their work still&nbsp;
provided insights into how organisms interact.&nbsp;

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:41.360
It’s like a story: incapable of holding the vast&nbsp;
entirety of our existence, and yet still very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:48.000
useful in its narrowness. That the consummate&nbsp;
model organism has such a consummate model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:57.840
predator then is quite fortunate for us, even&nbsp;
if for the microcosmos, it’s just another story.&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:04.880
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

